#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc1b94b-0505-5045-9c22-3e63a4a744d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494378} {:answer/id #uuid "21506373-dea4-5f4d-9f93-d7562e610ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+z-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494384} {:answer/id #uuid "2a47be68-d669-5229-a96b-5585cf274850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494398} {:answer/id #uuid "52e32d38-2f7f-54d4-aa22-472e7b380fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494484} {:answer/id #uuid "af02f3d5-bf22-51b9-93e1-cd648f20a6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af02f3d5-bf22-51b9-93e1-cd648f20a6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494725} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fcc8e6d-0fa3-50b3-a14c-039865378300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e41d6d16-a04e-52ec-b457-d59cbdbce646" :question/math-academy-id "q-127296" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(5,2,1)$ where $t=3,$ given that\n\n$$\nT(3)=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j),\\,N(3)=\\frac{1}{5}(4\\,i-3\\,j).\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(5,2,1)$ is $p=⟨5,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find the equation of the osculating plane, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to it. To do that, we find the cross product of the unit tangent $T(3)$ and the principal normal $N(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3)\\times N(3) & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\times \\frac{1}{5}(4\\,i-3\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(-25\\,k) \\\\  & =-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our perpendicular vector $n$ is \n\n$$\nn=T(3)\\times N(3)=-k=⟨0,0,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a general point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩ & =⟨5,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩ \\\\ x(0)+y(0)+z(-1) & =5(0)+2(0)+1(-1) \\\\ 0+0-z & =0+0-1 \\\\ z-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1cb0c5a-9862-57eb-b57f-acb23890d677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375869} {:answer/id #uuid "62e36acb-5f50-5c64-b175-8079a61023a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375870} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dbbdb6-befc-5ff1-81d4-cba020f3cb12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375871} {:answer/id #uuid "13addc19-500d-5e0f-acbb-8b63b95f8faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375872} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2fae7f-e95c-5979-8992-c80d112c68aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2fae7f-e95c-5979-8992-c80d112c68aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375873} :answer-field/id #uuid "6215ee61-981f-5e63-acd6-8f36d8ca0439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1f2ddde-40be-5a46-987b-92cfeb3c2c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-127321" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-1⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1⟩=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},0⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|0+0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68feb21c-0d21-5889-8d1e-2eb4b27382af" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f4a1cf-1d9c-52c0-93f9-fac0c9f4424a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+2y-2z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494359} {:answer/id #uuid "4cee8c19-9c9a-577e-a3f8-d619d6283b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+2z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494474} {:answer/id #uuid "7d23c0e8-53a9-5056-a1fd-94cac249f356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494587} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6b9e01-3c1a-52fe-ac8e-85d3b31c3694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494719} {:answer/id #uuid "f999fad7-5ac9-5a6c-b11f-a8d5b17ebd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y+2z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad6b9e01-3c1a-52fe-ac8e-85d3b31c3694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y-2z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494719} :answer-field/id #uuid "221fe3b5-df38-59f4-9697-23497b6f9422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "930676c3-f1de-5f64-b229-4f0536fe23c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-33681" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=1,$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =-6t\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}(-2\\,i+2t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\ N(t) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+2}[2t\\,i+(2-{t}^{2})\\,j+2t\\,k].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=1$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+2}(-2\\,i+2(1)\\,j+(1)^{2}\\,k)=\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+2\\,j+k) \\\\ N(1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+2}[2(1)\\,i+(2-(1)^{2})\\,j+2(1)\\,k]=\\frac{1}{3}[2\\,i+j+2\\,k].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=1$ is\n\n$$\np=r(1)=-6(1)\\,i+3(1)^{2}\\,j+(1)^{3}\\,k=⟨-6,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(1)$ and the principal normal $N(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\times N(1) & =(\\frac{1}{3}(-2\\,i+2\\,j+k))\\times (\\frac{1}{3}[2\\,i+j+2\\,k]) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(3\\,i+6\\,j-6\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=3\\cdot (T(1)\\times N(1))=i+2\\,j-2\\,k=⟨1,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,-2⟩ & =⟨-6,3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,-2⟩ \\\\ 1\\cdot x+2\\cdot y+(-2)\\cdot z & =-2 \\\\ x+2y-2z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95f2e191-0760-5cb8-b55a-cd2955150e82" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of an Osculating Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab0962f-47a2-56ad-a8f4-fcde0f2c7c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\frac{3s}{5}\\,i+(1+u)\\,j+\\frac{4s}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494439} {:answer/id #uuid "5de6ef11-b9ca-5631-a3cc-f5b59a304c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=3s\\,i+u\\,j+4s\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494510} {:answer/id #uuid "705e03a6-9ab8-53c5-9678-5309da677239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=3s\\,i+\\,j+4s\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494557} {:answer/id #uuid "8a98657a-2147-5b2d-935a-70a6bbecad00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\frac{4s}{5}\\,i+u\\,j+\\frac{3s}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494630} {:answer/id #uuid "e3305b27-e0f7-5fa3-b438-d590ded22286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=4s\\,i+(u+1)\\,j+3s\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab0962f-47a2-56ad-a8f4-fcde0f2c7c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\frac{3s}{5}\\,i+(1+u)\\,j+\\frac{4s}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494439} :answer-field/id #uuid "23404bd6-4bc0-57cc-bda2-65593c06a2bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5739684-baa6-5731-83f0-2d9751d3974c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78208" :question/problem "Find the equation of the osculating plane (in parametric form) for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,1,0)$ where $t=1$, given that\n\n$$\nT(1)=\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,k),\\,N(1)=j.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(0,1,0)$ is $r(1)=j.$\n\nThe osculating plane passes through the point that is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ The vectors $T(1)$ and $N(1)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, the equation of the plane (in parametric form) can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(1)+s\\,T(1)+u\\,N(1),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the osculating plane at the point $(0,1,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(1)+s\\,T(1)+u\\,N(1) \\\\  & =j+\\frac{s}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,k)+u(j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3s}{5}\\,i+(1+u)\\,j+\\frac{4s}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b22863-c898-5ed0-b7a4-d5118be485ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494452} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1d36f3-9602-5457-a0d0-8601d6174039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494476} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa940d7-5d61-5c5f-b788-1ed2ba0daa1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494556} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c77738-63e6-5353-aa74-2d3811b0e015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494701} {:answer/id #uuid "ff84a3b3-575b-5c27-8da1-c81c5b9aec3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e1d36f3-9602-5457-a0d0-8601d6174039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494476} :answer-field/id #uuid "491651a6-ff0e-5562-b9d4-4eca920b2b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b71da475-641a-50cd-8186-88383b78b5a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-79232" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,3,2)$ where $t=-1$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-1)=k,\\,N(-1)=i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(1,3,2)$ is $p=i+3\\,j+2\\,k.$\n\nWe need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(-1)$ and the principal normal $N(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(-1)\\times N(-1) \\\\  & =k\\times i \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|(i+3\\,j+2\\,k)\\cdot j| \\\\  & =|0+3+0| \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
